<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Start Monday. | Canada2080"
    description="Greg Hart on future-ready places and relentless pragmatism at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Capacity · 1:55 p.m."
    headline="Start Monday."
    name="Greg Hart"
    bio="Greg Hart is co-founder and facilitator at InceptionU, founder of Future Fit Cities, and a member of the TransPod Advisory Board. His work connects systems thinking and strategy to practical implementation."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/greg-hart-design/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5417.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Greg Hart speaking beside the podium. His name is on the slide."
    photos={photos}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Stop treating 2080 as a problem to be solved. Strategy asks what could
        be created, then builds the conditions.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Routine problems should be solved. This one, he said, is not a
            wicked problem. If it is framed that way, it is not really a
            problem at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            He used a cargo boat full of containers as a picture of old
            assumptions carried into the future. He named VUCA, volatility,
            uncertainty, complexity, and ambiguity, and he cited Sidney Dekker
            on the drift into failure: a slow collapse that arrives because the
            old thinking was kept too long. If the boat leaks, look for a new
            boat. Systems are not broken, he said. They produce patterns we
            dislike.
        </li>
        <li>
            Design, in his definition, is creating the conditions for success,
            the soil the work grows in. Speed is a byproduct of low friction,
            not a goal. He pointed to the Apollo teams as a mission shaped
            around potential.
        </li>
        <li>
            A living-systems test: life creates the conditions for more life.
            Potential and relationships over raw quantity. Quality over
            quantity. Lower energy when it will do. Keep operating, and still
            invest in the next option.
        </li>
        <li>
            Places matter. A Utrecht highway, built as an expressway around
            1980, was returned to a canal. Fail to design the place, he said,
            and the result is a Blade Runner future.
        </li>
        <li>
            Education, in his view, is outdated for this work. Rewire it around
            critical thinking, systems thinking, and computational thinking,
            not memorized facts.
        </li>
        <li>
            Relentless pragmatism: know where you are going, know where you are
            without romance, and do the actions in between.
        </li>
    </ul>
</KickoffSession>
